## 3. Results and discussion
Two new three-dimensional porous $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ based metal–organic frameworks, $[\mathsf{C d}_{2}(\mathsf{o b a})_{2}(4\mathsf{-b p d b})_{2}]_{n}{\cdot}3.5(\mathsf{D M F})$ (TMU-8) and $[\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{o b a})(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy})]_{n^{\prime}}(\mathsf{D M F})$ (TMU-9) have been readily and quickly synthesized by sonochemical process of a mixture of $\mathrm{Cd}({\mathrm{OAc}})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\operatorname{H}_{2}{\mathsf{o b a}}$ , and $^4$ -bpdb or $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy. Comparison between the simulated (derived from the single crystal structures of TMU-8 and TMU-9) and experimental (resulting from the sonochemical synthesized powder) powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns confirm that nano plates of TMU-8 and TMU-9 were structurally identical to TMU-8 and TMU-9 prepared via conventional heating (Fig. 1) (as also evidenced by elemental analysis).
The IR spectra of sonochemically synthesized TMU-8 and TMU-9 are similar to those synthesized by conventional heating.
Both of them show the symmetric $\nu_{\mathrm{sym}}(\mathsf{C O O})$ and asymmetric $\nu_{a s}(C O O)$ vibrations of the carboxylate groups at $1409\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1604\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. Also the characteristic absorption peak $\mathrm{{'}}\nu_{\mathrm{C=0}}=1679\mathrm{{cm}^{-1},}$ ) of DMF is present (Fig. 2).
In TMU-8 the oba ligands bind $\operatorname{Cd}_{2}$ units and form 2D sheets that are connected through the linear 4-bpdb, to form a three-dimensional framework. TMU-8 consists of two channels along the $a$ -axis (aperture size: $9.3\times3.9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $6.7\times3.0\mathrm{~\AA}$ , $44.3\%$ void space per unit cell) [35] which are occupied by DMF guest molecules (Fig. 3). Also, TMU-9 contains narrow channels along $a-$ and $b$ -axes (aperture size $8.2\times1.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $10.9\times2.9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , respectively; $27.8\%$ void space per unit cell) (Fig. 4) [35].
The morphology and size of TMU-8 and TMU-9 were investigated using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) by changing three parameters; sonication time, sonication power and concentration of starting materials as well as control of nucleation. Almost in all cases nanoplate was the dominant morphology.
For TMU-8, sonication time as a parameter was changed at a constant concentration of [0.025] M of starting materials.
Nanoplates of TMU-8 were obtained for all three sonication time (30, 60 and $90\mathrm{min}\mathrm{.}$ ). For this MOF, thickness of plates is grown from $67\mathrm{nm}$ to $130\mathrm{nm}$ upon increasing the sonication time (Fig. 5). Therefore in sonication time of $30\mathrm{min}$ , concentration of initial reagents was changed to half and twice the first concentration ([0.0125] M, and [0.05] M). In this case thinner plates with thickness ranging from 25 to $60\mathrm{nm}$ were collected at lower concentrations (Fig. 6a and b). Also thinner plates (thickness of ${\sim}80\mathrm{nm}$ ) have been acquired upon using TEA in reaction with condition of $30\mathrm{min}$ and concentration of [0.025] M (Fig. 6c). This means in this case fast nucleation has the same effect as reduction of concentrations. Finally, in order to find out the effect of ultrasonic power on morphology and size of TMU-8, experimental condition of best sample (sample E) was set at ultrasonic power of 36W. In this case uniformly sheet-like morphology with thickness of $150{-}200\mathrm{nm}$ has been obtained (Fig. 6d).
A similar process was carried out for TMU-9 on same concentration of initial reagents. Results show that rod-shaped TMU-9 was obtained under these conditions in which the rods are made of nano-sheets and the thickness of plates are reduced from $250\mathrm{nm}$ to $70\mathrm{nm}$ at longer sonication times (Fig. 7). Then in sonication time of $90\mathrm{min}$ , the role of concentration was investigated by choosing [0.05] M and [0.0125] M of initial reagents. For TMU-9, reduction of concentration leads to uniformly narrow nanoplates unlike those obtained upon using higher concentrations where non-uniform plates were acquired (Fig. 8a and b). Finally, the effect of using TEA on morphology and size of TMU-9 was investigated in
30 and $90\mathrm{min}$ of sonication times. The SEM images show the formation of separate nanoplates in which plates are more uniform in a shorter reaction times (Fig. 8c and d). In the case of TMU-9 increasing the ultrasonic power has led to nanoparticles ranging from 20 to $100\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 9).
To prepare cadmium oxide nanoparticles, these MOFs were calcined at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Fig. 10 depicts the XRD patterns of the residue obtained from calcination of TMU-8 and TMU-9 nanoplates.
The Bragg diffraction peaks in the range of $2\theta=20{-}80^{\circ}$ exhibit the typical patterns of the cubic monteponite structure of CdO (JCPDS No. 05-0640, $a=4.69\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ).
SEM images of residue obtained from the direct calcination show the formation of aggregation of CdO nanoparticle in the range of $60{-}160\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 11). Particle sizes of CdO obtained from calcination of TMU-9 are smaller than those obtained from calcination of TMU-8. This may be related to the structures of these two MOFs that influence the final morphologies of CdO particles [32].
$[\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{o b a})(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy})]_{n}\cdot(\mathsf{D M F})_{y}$ (TMU-9) were prepared by ultrasonic irradiation and various parameters such as time and concentration of initial reagents were investigated. Results show that lower concentration of reagents leads to decrease in particle size. Moreover, triethylamine influences the speed of nucleation during the synthesis and decreases particle size. Meanwhile, increasing power of ultrasonic irradiation led to uniformly sheet-like morphology for TMU-8 and nanoparticles ranging from 20 to $100\mathrm{nm}$ in the case of TMU-9. Finally, by thermolysis of these MOFs at $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under air atmosphere CdO nanoparticles were obtained.
